(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-anti-infectives-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Ocular Delivery & Anti-infectives Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Erythromycin ointment and azithromycin solution share a mechanism. What is it?","choices":["Inhibition of protein synthesis at the 30S ribosomal subunit","Inhibition of DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV","Inhibition of protein synthesis at the 50S ribosomal subunit","Inhibition of cell wall mucopeptide transfer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The macrolides block transpeptidation at the 50S subunit, so RNA-dependent protein synthesis stops and the organism dies. Knowing the subunit is what separates them from the aminoglycosides. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is specific enough to an ophthalmic antibiotic class to be worth knowing, rather than being shared by all of them?","choices":["Ocular irritation with erythromycin","Corneal ulceration from an aminoglycoside","Hypersensitivity with bacitracin","Unpleasant taste after a macrolide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ocular irritation is common to essentially every agent here and so distinguishes nothing — but aminoglycosides used for several days can produce corneal ulceration and a reactive keratoconjunctivitis, which changes how long you continue them. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Roughly one in six patients given a particular ophthalmic fluoroquinolone develops a visible finding on the eye. Which drug, and what is seen?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin, and a white precipitate forms","Moxifloxacin, and a white precipitate forms","Gatifloxacin, and corneal deposits form","Ofloxacin, and conjunctival pigmentation develops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It occurs in about seventeen percent, and it is worth recognizing so it is not mistaken for a worsening infiltrate or a new ulcer. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Latanoprost and dipivefrin are both given in an inactive form. What does the eye do with them?","choices":["It concentrates them in the ciliary body without altering them","It excretes them unchanged into the aqueous humor","It binds them to tear proteins until they are displaced","It metabolizes them — they are prodrugs activated in the eye"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Some drugs are metabolized within the eye itself, which is exploited deliberately: dipivefrin becomes epinephrine and latanoprost becomes prostaglandin F2-alpha once inside. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Ganciclovir treats herpetic keratitis topically and cytomegalovirus retinitis by intravitreal injection. How does it work?","choices":["It inhibits thymidylate synthetase and substitutes for thymidine","It competitively inhibits DNA polymerase","It binds sterols in the viral envelope","It inhibits viral protease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Competing with the natural nucleotide at DNA polymerase halts viral DNA synthesis, and the intravitreal route is what makes it usable for retinitis. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"When are cultures routinely taken before starting an ophthalmic antibiotic?","choices":["Not unless an unusual organism is expected","Always, because resistance is emerging","Only for conjunctivitis, not for keratitis","Only when the patient wears contact lenses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Broad-spectrum topical therapy is started empirically. Cultures are reserved for when something unusual is likely — the immunocompromised patient is the example given. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why has the microbiological spectrum of ocular infection shifted over time, by the example given?","choices":["Contact lens use increased Pseudomonas","Fluoroquinolone use selected for resistant Staphylococcus","Vaccination reduced Haemophilus influenzae","Topical steroid use increased fungal infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The spectrum is not fixed — the example given is the fall in Haemophilus influenzae after the vaccine was introduced, which changes what empiric therapy has to cover. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Absorption can be deliberately increased by blocking the tear ducts with silicone plugs or cautery. Why does that work?","choices":["It stops the drop draining away","It increases tear production and dilutes irritants","It reduces drug binding to tear proteins","It lowers the corneal epithelial barrier"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nasolacrimal drainage removes drug from the eye and sends it systemically. Blocking it leaves more drug in contact with the cornea for longer — the same goal as thickening the formulation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How does natamycin act on fungi?","choices":["It inhibits fungal cell wall glucan synthesis","It binds sterol and increases fungal cell membrane permeability","It inhibits ergosterol synthesis at 14-alpha-demethylase","It inhibits fungal DNA polymerase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Binding membrane sterol makes the fungal membrane leaky. It is a polyene, and that is the polyene mechanism. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Gentamicin and tobramycin act on the bacterial ribosome. At which subunit?","choices":["The 50S subunit","Both the 30S and 50S subunits equally","The 30S subunit","Neither — they act on the cell membrane"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The aminoglycosides bind the 30S subunit and interfere with protein synthesis. The macrolides take the 50S, and keeping the two numbers straight is the whole distinction. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Polytrim combines two agents. How does each one work?","choices":["Trimethoprim antagonizes p-aminobenzoic acid; polymyxin B blocks the 50S ribosome","Trimethoprim blocks folate reduction; polymyxin B disrupts the bacterial membrane","Both inhibit bacterial cell wall synthesis","Trimethoprim disrupts the membrane; polymyxin B blocks folate reduction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Trimethoprim stops folic acid becoming tetrahydrofolate, so nucleic acid and protein production fail. Polymyxin B binds membrane phospholipids and lets intracellular contents leak out. Two unrelated mechanisms in one bottle. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Azithromycin solution is dosed twice daily while erythromycin ointment may be needed four or more times a day. Why is azithromycin still not the usual choice?","choices":["It is considerably more expensive","It is less effective against the usual conjunctivitis organisms","It cannot be used in contact lens wearers","It causes a white precipitate on the eye"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The dosing advantage is real and helps compliance, but cost keeps it from being used as often clinically. Erythromycin remains the common one. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A patient presents with an eye complaint and you are about to instill a medication. What must be done first?","choices":["Measure intraocular pressure","Measure visual acuity","Instill fluorescein to exclude an epithelial defect","Confirm the patient is not wearing contact lenses"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acuity is measured before anything goes in the eye, and again at every follow-up. If it worsens, that is an immediate ophthalmology consult — which is the whole reason a baseline has to exist. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A small, mild, peripheral ocular infection is being treated. What is the usual approach, and what limits it?","choices":["Topical therapy, needing frequent dosing","Oral therapy, limited by first-pass metabolism","Intravitreal injection, limited by the risk of endophthalmitis","Subconjunctival injection, limited by local tissue injury"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical gives very high local concentrations, which is the point, but bioavailability beyond the eye is poor and drops have to be given often. Broad-spectrum agents are used empirically without cultures unless an unusual organism is expected. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Besides Pseudomonas cover, what is the other indication for which fluoroquinolones are specifically preferred?","choices":["Blepharitis","Ophthalmia neonatorum prophylaxis","Fungal keratitis","Corneal ulcers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Corneal ulcers and suspected Pseudomonas are the two the fluoroquinolones are named for. Against that sit the cost and the emerging resistance, which is why they are not first for everything. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How do the ophthalmic fluoroquinolones kill bacteria?","choices":["They inhibit RNA-dependent protein synthesis at the 50S ribosome","They block folic acid synthesis","They bind sterols and increase membrane permeability","They inhibit DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blocking those enzymes prevents supercoiled DNA relaxing, and the strands break. This is the class used for corneal ulcers and for suspected Pseudomonas. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A contact lens wearer has bacterial conjunctivitis and keratitis has been ruled out. Which class is preferred, and why?","choices":["A macrolide, because erythromycin is soothing on an inflamed eye","An aminoglycoside, because it covers gram-negative organisms","Sulfacetamide, because it is inexpensive and well tolerated","A fluoroquinolone, for Pseudomonas cover"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Contact lens use raises the risk of Pseudomonas aeruginosa, a gram-negative rod, and the fluoroquinolones are the class that covers it. They are also the preferred choice for corneal ulcers for the same reason. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which factors determine whether an ocular infection is treated topically or with oral or parenteral agents?","choices":["The organism's Gram stain alone","The patient's visual acuity alone","Setting, age, immune status and extent","Whether the patient wears contact lenses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those four decide the route. Preseptal disease may be manageable topically or orally, while postseptal or orbital cellulitis is a different proposition entirely. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A drop instilled in the eye reaches the systemic circulation and causes a side effect elsewhere in the body. By which route did it get there, and what is the consequence for its metabolism?","choices":["Transcorneal absorption, which avoids first-pass metabolism","Nasolacrimal drainage, with extensive first-pass metabolism","Nasolacrimal drainage, which avoids first-pass metabolism","Conjunctival absorption into the retinal circulation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drug draining through the nasolacrimal duct is absorbed across nasal mucosa straight into the systemic circulation, bypassing the liver's first pass. That is why a topical drop can produce a systemic effect out of proportion to its dose. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Transcorneal absorption has a lag time, and its rate depends most on one variable. Which, and what law governs it?","choices":["The concentration gradient, governed by Fick's law","The tear film pH, governed by the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation","The corneal blood flow, governed by Fick's principle","The drug's molecular weight, governed by Graham's law"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Transcorneal movement is passive diffusion, so the steeper the gradient the faster the transfer. That is also why raising concentration, rather than volume, is the lever that matters. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the stated goal of treating infectious conjunctivitis?","choices":["Relieve symptoms while the infection resolves on its own","Prevent transmission to the other eye","Establish the organism by culture","Eradicate the infection and prevent long-term complications"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both halves matter — clearing the organism is not the whole aim, because the reason to treat promptly is what an untreated infection leaves behind. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 13"}]};
